---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "How to Dispute a Total-Loss Valuation | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "A factual, evidence-based process for disputing an insurer's total-loss valuation — what to gather, how to write the request, and when to escalate.";
const path = "/guides/total-loss/how-to-dispute-a-total-loss-valuation/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="How to Dispute a Total-Loss Valuation Factually"
  cluster={{ name: "Total Loss", href: "/guides/total-loss/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-07-29"
  reviewedDate="2026-07-29"
  summary="A strong dispute is built on specific factual and arithmetic points, not a general feeling the offer is low. Here's a step-by-step process, plus when the policy's appraisal clause is worth considering."
  sources={[
    'General description of the appraisal-clause process (each side selects an independent appraiser; if they disagree, a neutral umpire decides) is a common auto-policy provision, but its exact wording, availability, and scope for diminished-value claims varies by insurer and state — always read your own policy\'s actual appraisal language before relying on this description.',
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Total-Loss Offer Audit calculator", href: "/calculators/total-loss-offer-audit/" },
    { label: "Claim Letter & Evidence Packet Builder", href: "/calculators/claim-letter-builder/" },
    { label: "When to Consider an Independent Appraisal or Attorney", href: "/guides/claim-process/when-to-consider-an-independent-appraisal-or-attorney/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Step 1: Get the full valuation report in writing</h2>
  <p>
    If you only received a summary letter, request the complete underlying report — the one
    showing comparable vehicles and adjustments (see
    <a href="/guides/total-loss/reading-your-total-loss-valuation-report/">Reading Your Total-Loss
    Valuation Report</a>). You can't dispute specifics you haven't seen.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: Check the facts, not just the number</h2>
  <p>
    Verify your vehicle's description (trim, mileage, options), then check each comparable for
    year, mileage, trim, distance, and listing status against the categories in
    <a href="/guides/total-loss/common-valuation-report-errors/">Common Valuation Report Errors</a>.
    Run everything through the
    <a href="/calculators/total-loss-offer-audit/">Total-Loss Offer Audit calculator</a> so the
    statistics and comp-quality review are documented, not just a gut feeling.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3: Gather your own comparables</h2>
  <p>
    Find at least two, ideally three or more, real listings following
    <a href="/guides/total-loss/finding-your-own-comparable-vehicles/">the comp-finding guide</a>,
    with URLs, dates checked, prices, mileage, and trim recorded.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4: Write a specific, factual request</h2>
  <p>
    A factual correction request names the exact fact you believe is wrong (a comp's trim, a
    missing option, a mileage figure) and the evidence supporting the correction — not a general
    complaint that the number "feels low." The
    <a href="/calculators/claim-letter-builder/">Claim Letter Builder</a> has templates for both a
    factual correction request and a comparable-vehicle reconsideration request.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    <p>
      <strong>What a specific dispute point looks like:</strong> "Comparable #2 in your report is
      listed as SE trim; the actual listing at [URL], checked [date], is for an SEL trim with
      navigation and leather, which are not present on the base SE. Please re-run this comparable
      with the correct trim, or substitute a genuine SE-trim comparable."
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Step 5: Know when the appraisal clause applies</h2>
  <p>
    Many auto policies include an appraisal clause: each side picks an independent appraiser, and if
    those two disagree, a neutral umpire resolves the difference — with the umpire's decision (or
    agreement between any two of the three) typically binding. This process exists specifically for
    value disputes, but its exact availability and wording depends on your policy — read yours, or
    ask your insurer directly whether it applies to your claim type.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you whether your specific dispute will succeed, what your state's process is if
    the insurer refuses to budge, or whether your policy's appraisal clause applies to your
    situation — those all require reading your own policy and, if needed, talking to your state
    insurance department or a licensed attorney.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
